# Release trains (required)

Classify the change first, then branch off that train. See
[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) **Release trains**.

While a patch and a minor are in flight: **patch** = `main` (fixes,
refactors, improvements); **minor** = `release/X.Y.0` (new features).
Never land minor features on `main` until the patch is tagged. After
that tag, `main` is the minor train.

**Current trains** live in [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) **Release
trains** (update that snippet only).

Split mixed PRs (refactor + feature): patch first, then minor. If the
refactor has no standalone value, keep both on the minor train. Never
put a feature on the patch train. If still ambiguous, use the patch
train unless the change adds user-facing capability.

## Changelog

`## [Unreleased]` is per-branch (patch on `main`, minor on
`release/X.Y.0`). Dated sections stay frozen.

## Cherry-picks

Cherry-pick each patch fix from `main` onto `release/X.Y.0` (the commit
that landed the change on `main`). On conflict, keep minor feature code
and re-apply the fix; if it still will not apply, abort and PR a port to
`release/X.Y.0` — do not change `main` to match minor-only APIs.
Hotfixes that must ship on both trains land on `main` first, then
cherry-pick immediately.

When merging `release/X.Y.0` into `main`, prefer `main` for patch fixes
on a shared surface and `release/X.Y.0` for minor-only APIs (see
CONTRIBUTING **Cherry-picks and merge-backs**). If `main` diverged,
cherry-pick remaining patch commits onto `release/X.Y.0` first; do not
force-push `main`. Tag from `main` (patch tags the completing commit on
`main`; minor merges `release/X.Y.0` into `main` first).

Version bump still happens on the PR that completes that version.
